<h1>Conveyor Safety Guide</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Conveyor safety in the U.S. rests on a few OSHA rules and one consensus standard. OSHA 29 CFR 1910.212 requires machine guarding against hazards such as ingoing nip points and rotating parts. 1910.219 covers shafts, pulleys, belts, chains, and gears. 1910.147 covers lockout/tagout. ASME B20.1 is the conveyor-specific safety standard for design, construction, installation, maintenance, inspection, and operation. This guide covers what each requires, how to guard shafts and nip points, where e-stops and pull cords go, and how to lock out a conveyor.</p>
<h2 id="standards-heading">Safety Standards</h2>
<p>Several standards overlap on conveyors. Know which one applies and who enforces it.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Standard</th><th>Type</th><th>What it covers on a conveyor</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>OSHA 29 CFR 1910.212</td><td>Federal regulation, general industry</td><td>Machine guarding for point of operation, ingoing nip points, rotating parts</td></tr>
<tr><td>OSHA 29 CFR 1910.219</td><td>Federal regulation, general industry</td><td>Shafting, pulleys, belts, chains, sprockets, gears, couplings</td></tr>
<tr><td>OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147</td><td>Federal regulation, general industry</td><td>Lockout/tagout during servicing and maintenance</td></tr>
<tr><td>OSHA 29 CFR 1926.555</td><td>Federal regulation, construction</td><td>Conveyors used in construction work</td></tr>
<tr><td>ASME B20.1</td><td>Consensus standard</td><td>Conveyor-specific safety requirements</td></tr>
<tr><td>NFPA 79</td><td>Consensus standard</td><td>Electrical equipment of industrial machinery, including e-stop devices</td></tr>
<tr><td>ANSI B11.19</td><td>Consensus standard</td><td>Performance requirements for safeguarding and other risk reduction measures</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3><a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.212" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.212</a>, General Requirements for All Machines</h3>
<p>Paragraph (a)(1) requires one or more methods of machine guarding to protect the operator and other employees from hazards such as those created by the point of operation, ingoing nip points, rotating parts, and flying chips and sparks. Paragraph (a)(2) requires guards to be affixed to the machine where possible and not to create a hazard themselves. On conveyors, this is the rule that covers belt-to-pulley and chain-to-sprocket nip points. Machine guarding (1910.212) was number 10 on <a href="https://www.osha.gov/top10citedstandards" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA's most frequently cited standards</a> for fiscal year 2025.</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.219" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.219</a>, Mechanical Power-Transmission Apparatus</h3>
<p>This regulation covers the drive train: shafting, pulleys, belts, chains and sprockets, gears, and couplings. Its height rule is the one most conveyor layouts must check. Exposed horizontal shafting and pulleys 7 feet or less from the floor or working platform must be guarded. The shaft guarding section below lists the specific requirements.</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.147" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147</a>, Control of Hazardous Energy (Lockout/Tagout)</h3>
<p>Employers must develop, document, and use energy control procedures before employees service or maintain machines, including conveyors. The standard allows a narrow exception from documenting a procedure when eight listed conditions are all met. Procedures must be inspected at least annually by an authorized employee other than the one using the procedure. Lockout/tagout was number 4 on OSHA's FY 2025 most-cited list.</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1926/1926.555" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 29 CFR 1926.555</a>, Conveyors (Construction)</h3>
<p>For conveyors used in construction work, 1926.555 requires a means of stopping the motor at the operator's station, an audible warning signal sounded immediately before start-up, emergency stop switches that must be reset before the conveyor can restart, guards where conveyors pass over work areas or aisles, and lockout and "Do Not Operate" tags during repairs. Paragraph (a)(8) requires conveyors to meet ANSI B20.1-1957. In general industry, 1910.212 and 1910.219 apply instead.</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>, Safety Standard for Conveyors and Related Equipment</h3>
<p>ASME describes B20.1 as applying to "the design, construction, installation, maintenance, inspection, and operation of conveyors and conveying systems in relation to hazards." It covers bulk material, package, and unit handling conveyors in permanent, temporary, or portable use, and excludes conveyors for moving people. The current edition is B20.1-2024. It is a voluntary consensus standard in general industry, but it is the document conveyor suppliers design to, so name the edition in your RFQ.</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.nfpa.org/codes-and-standards/nfpa-79-standard-development/79" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NFPA 79</a>, Electrical Standard for Industrial Machinery</h3>
<p>NFPA 79 covers machine electrical equipment: wiring, control circuits, disconnects, and emergency stop devices. The 2018 edition of B20.1, as summarized in <a href="https://cdn.intertek.com/www-intertek-com/dms-legacy/ASME-B20-1-Rev-6-22-2018-ED-6-22-2020.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intertek's B20.1 standards update notice</a>, requires conveyor electrical installations to conform to the National Electrical Code (Article 670 and other applicable articles) and NFPA 79.</p>
<h3><a href="https://www.b11standards.org/storeitems/ansi-b1119" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ANSI B11.19</a>, Performance Requirements for Safeguarding</h3>
<p>B11.19 sets performance requirements for the design, construction, installation, operation, and maintenance of risk reduction measures: inherently safe design, guards, control functions, protective devices, and administrative controls. It does not tell you which measure to pick. That choice comes from the risk assessment. It is a useful reference when a conveyor is integrated with packaging machinery covered by other B11 standards.</p>
<h2 id="shaft-guarding">Shaft and Pinch Point Guarding: Best Practices</h2>
<p>Conveyor shaft and nip point guarding best practices, drawn from the OSHA rules and guidance cited on this page:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Guard every in-running nip point and rotating part.</strong> 1910.212(a)(1) requires guarding against ingoing nip points and rotating parts: head and tail pulleys, snub rollers, chain and sprocket drives, and transfers.</li>
<li><strong>Enclose low shafting and pulleys.</strong> 1910.219 requires guarding of exposed horizontal shafting and pulleys 7 feet or less from the floor or working platform.</li>
<li><strong>Finish shaft ends and keyways.</strong> Trim projecting shaft ends to no more than one-half the shaft diameter or cover them with nonrotating caps or safety sleeves, and fill or cover unused keyways (1910.219(c)(4)).</li>
<li><strong>Use fixed guards first, interlocked guards where access is routine.</strong> 1910.212(a)(2) requires guards affixed to the machine where possible that do not create new hazards. Guards removed for cleaning or jam clearing should be interlocked.</li>
<li><strong>Treat e-stops and pull cords as backup.</strong> OSHA 3170 notes that emergency stops do not prevent exposure, so they never replace a guard.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rotating shafts and in-running nip points cause most conveyor entanglement injuries. OSHA's <a href="https://www.osha.gov/etools/machine-guarding/introduction/hazardous-motions-actions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Machine Guarding eTool</a> warns that "even smooth, slowly rotating shafts can grip hair and clothing," and that the danger increases when set screws, bolts, or keys project from the rotating part. OSHA's <a href="https://www.osha.gov/sites/default/files/publications/OSHA3170.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">amputation guide (OSHA 3170)</a> lists the belt conveyor locations that need attention: take-up and discharge ends, where the belt or chain enters or exits the in-going nip point, where the belt wraps around pulleys, snub rollers where the belt changes direction, where conveyors adjoin, and transfers or deflectors.</p>
<h3>What 1910.219 Requires for Shafts</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Horizontal shafting.</strong> Exposed horizontal shafting 7 feet or less from the floor or working platform must be enclosed by a stationary casing, or by a trough enclosing the sides and top or sides and bottom as location requires (1910.219(c)(2)(i)).</li>
<li><strong>Projecting shaft ends.</strong> Shaft ends must have a smooth edge and end and may not project more than one-half the shaft diameter unless guarded by nonrotating caps or safety sleeves (1910.219(c)(4)(i)).</li>
<li><strong>Keyways.</strong> Unused keyways must be filled or covered (1910.219(c)(4)(ii)).</li>
<li><strong>Pulleys.</strong> Pulleys with any part 7 feet or less from the floor or working platform must be guarded (1910.219(d)(1)).</li>
</ul>
<h3>Guarding Checklist by Location</h3>
