<h3>Guard Design</h3>
<p>Guards may be barriers, enclosures, grating, fences, or other obstructions that prevent contact with moving parts, per OSHA 3170. Guard openings should be small enough to prevent employees from reaching danger areas, and guards must not create new pinch or shear points. For power presses, 1910.217 Table O-10 sets maximum guard openings by distance from the hazard. It is not written for conveyors, but it is a useful reference for opening size. Fixed guards that need a tool to remove are standard practice. Guards that must come off for routine cleaning or jam clearing should be interlocked instead.</p>
<p>OSHA 3170 also lists secondary methods, such as safeguarding by distance (location), railings, warning signs, and pre-start signals. Use these where a primary guard is not feasible, not as a substitute where it is. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/">conveyor design guide</a> covers how to build guarding into the layout from the start.</p>
<h3>Interlocked Guards</h3>
<p>An interlocked guard connects to the safety circuit through a safety switch. Opening the guard stops the conveyor, and the conveyor cannot restart until the guard is closed and a reset is given. Use interlocked guards where operators need frequent access for cleaning, changeover, or jam clearing. Choose interlock types that are hard to defeat, such as coded magnetic, RFID-coded, or trapped-key switches.</p>
<h2 id="estop-heading">Emergency Stop Systems</h2>
<p>OSHA 3170 notes that emergency stop devices are not safeguarding. They neither detect nor prevent exposure. They stop hazardous motion after someone recognizes a hazard. Guards come first. E-stops are the backup.</p>
<h3>Where B20.1 Requires E-Stops</h3>
<p>The B20.1 text quoted in Intertek's update notice (2018 edition) requires emergency stop buttons, pull cords, limit switches, or similar devices on remotely and automatically controlled conveyors, and where operator stations are unmanned or beyond voice or visual contact. They go in drive areas, loading areas, transfer points, and other potentially hazardous locations not guarded by location, position, or guards. Stop or emergency stop pushbuttons go at each operator control station. Each e-stop must act directly on the control of its conveyor, must not depend on stopping other equipment, and must not be overridable from other locations. That text does not set a fixed spacing in feet. Spacing comes from the layout and the risk assessment.</p>
<h3>E-Stop Button Requirements</h3>
<p>NFPA 79 and IEC 60204-1 require emergency stop actuators to be red, with a yellow background immediately around pushbuttons, and pushbuttons to be palm or mushroom-head type, as summarized by <a href="https://www.controldesign.com/safety/safety-components/article/55038147/e-stop-devices-can-take-many-forms" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Control Design</a>. E-stop devices latch in the stopped state and need a manual reset before operation resumes. OSHA 3170 recommends e-stop controls that require manual resetting, and 1926.555(a)(3) requires it for construction conveyors.</p>
<h3>Pull-Cord (Pull-Wire) Emergency Stops</h3>
<p>Pull-cord e-stops run a cable along the conveyor to cable-pull switches. Pulling the cable anywhere stops the conveyor. OSHA 3170 recommends emergency stop cables that run the entire length of continuously accessible conveyor belts. They are the practical choice wherever people work along a long run and discrete buttons would leave gaps. When conveyors are arranged in series, OSHA 3170 recommends that all stop automatically when one stops.</p>
<h3>Start-Up Warning</h3>
<p>Per the 2018 B20.1 text, a conveyor that is not fully visible from its control station, is automatically controlled, or is remotely controlled must not start until people in the area are alerted. Where starting could cause injury, an audible warning that can be heard at all hazardous points must sound for a set time before start-up. A flashing light may supplement or replace it where more effective. 1926.555(a)(1) requires an audible warning immediately before start-up on construction conveyors.</p>
<h3>Safety Circuit Architecture</h3>
<p>E-stop and interlock circuits should be fail-safe. A safety relay or safety PLC monitors e-stops, pull cords, guard interlocks, and safety sensors and controls the motor contactors or drive safe-torque-off input. Dual-channel wiring keeps a single fault from defeating the stop. The required Performance Level (ISO 13849-1) or SIL (IEC 62061) comes from the risk assessment, not from a blanket rule. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-parts/">conveyor parts guide</a> lists safety component categories.</p>
<h2 id="loto-heading">Lockout / Tagout</h2>
<p>Lockout/tagout (LOTO) isolates every energy source before maintenance, cleaning, or repair. OSHA 3170 includes a case where a worker removed a guard from a chain-and-sprocket drive without locking out, someone started the conveyor, and the worker's fingers were amputated.</p>
<h3>Energy Sources on Conveyors</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Electrical:</strong> the main disconnect feeds motors, controls, sensors, and heaters.</li>
<li><strong>Pneumatic:</strong> cylinders on diverters, pop-up transfers, and product stops keep stored air in the lines after the supply is shut off.</li>
<li><strong>Hydraulic:</strong> some heavy-duty drives hold pressurized fluid.</li>
<li><strong>Gravity:</strong> product on <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">incline conveyors</a> and vertical conveyors can roll or slide when the motor is off.</li>
<li><strong>Mechanical stored energy:</strong> springs in take-ups and counterweights on gravity take-ups.</li>
</ul>
<h3>LOTO Procedure Steps</h3>
<ol>
<li>Notify affected employees that the conveyor will be shut down.</li>
<li>Stop the conveyor with normal controls.</li>
<li>Operate every energy isolating device: electrical disconnects, pneumatic and hydraulic valves.</li>
<li>Apply a personal lock and tag to each isolating device. Each worker applies their own lock.</li>
<li>Release or restrain stored energy: bleed air lines, relieve hydraulic pressure, block gravity loads and take-ups.</li>
<li>Verify isolation by trying to start the conveyor with normal controls, then return controls to off.</li>
<li>Do the work.</li>
<li>Remove tools, reinstall guards, and confirm everyone is clear.</li>
<li>Each worker removes their own lock and tag. 1910.147(e)(3) requires removal by the employee who applied the device, with a documented exception when that employee is not available.</li>
<li>Notify affected employees and restart.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Jam Clearing and Minor Servicing</h3>
<p>OSHA 3170 explains that the 1910.147(a)(2)(ii) note allows an exception for minor servicing during normal production, with package jams as a common conveyor example. The alternative measures, such as opening a local disconnect, must be under the exclusive control of the employee clearing the jam so no one else can restart the conveyor without their knowledge. If clearing a jam exposes the worker to a nip point or other hazardous energy that the alternative measure does not control, full lockout applies.</p>
<h3>Group LOTO</h3>
<p>Under 1910.147(f)(3), group lockout must give each worker protection equivalent to a personal lock, with one primary authorized employee responsible for the crew. A common method is a group lockbox: isolation keys go in the box and each worker puts a personal lock on it. For broader maintenance practice, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-maintenance/">conveyor maintenance guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="training-heading">Training Requirements</h2>
<p>1910.147(c)(7) requires LOTO training for employees, retraining when job assignments, machines, or procedures change or when an inspection finds gaps, and certification of training with employee names and dates. OSHA 3170 recommends allowing only trained individuals to operate conveyors and only trained, authorized staff to service them.</p>
<h3>Operator Training</h3>
<p>Cover normal start and stop, e-stop and pull-cord locations, the jam-clearing procedure (never reach into a running conveyor), recognition of nip, pinch, and shear points, and the rule against removing or bypassing guards. OSHA 3170 also recommends banning loose clothing and jewelry near conveyors, requiring long hair to be secured, and forbidding riding on conveyors.</p>
<h3>Maintenance Training</h3>
<p>Maintenance staff need authorized-employee LOTO training, electrical safety training (NFPA 70E) where they work on energized equipment, fall protection for elevated conveyors, and machine-specific repair procedures. The annual periodic inspection under 1910.147(c)(6) includes a review of each authorized employee's responsibilities under the procedure being inspected.</p>
<h3>Signage and Warnings</h3>
<p>Signs and labels remind people of hazards at the point of risk. ANSI Z535 covers safety sign and label design. A good conveyor label states the hazard, the consequence, and how to avoid it. Place labels at nip points, drive access points, and maintenance access points, and replace faded or missing labels. Where B20.1 allows a warning sign in place of a timed start-up alarm, the sign must state that conveyors may start at any time and that personnel must keep clear.</p>
<h2 id="risk-heading">Risk Assessment</h2>
<p>A risk assessment identifies hazards, estimates severity and likelihood, and selects safeguards. ISO 12100 is the general machinery framework, and B20.1 itself relies on risk assessment in several clauses, including where e-stops and start-up warnings may be omitted. Do the assessment during the <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/">design phase</a> so safeguarding is built in rather than retrofitted.</p>
<p>Common conveyor hazards include nip points at pulleys and rollers, shear points at transfers, entanglement on rotating shafts and couplings, falling product, electrical hazards, and ergonomic strain from poor conveyor height. Each is rated for severity and probability, and the result sets the required Performance Level of the safety functions per ISO 13849-1.</p>
<p>Apply the hierarchy of controls: (1) eliminate the hazard by design, for example internal drives with no exposed nip points; (2) substitute a less hazardous option; (3) engineering controls such as guards, interlocks, and light curtains; (4) administrative controls such as procedures, training, and signs; (5) PPE last. Guards beat training. Eliminating the hazard beats guarding it. See the <a href="/conveyor-types/">conveyor types overview</a> for inherent safety differences between conveyor technologies.</p>
