<h2 id="faq-heading">Safety FAQ</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What OSHA standards apply to conveyor systems?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>1910.212 requires machine guarding against hazards such as ingoing nip points and rotating parts. 1910.219 covers shafts, pulleys, belts, chains, sprockets, gears, and couplings, including guarding of shafting and pulleys 7 feet or less from the floor or platform. 1910.147 requires documented lockout/tagout procedures. For construction work, 1926.555 covers conveyors. ASME B20.1 is the conveyor-specific consensus safety standard.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a conveyor nip point and why is it dangerous?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A nip point is where a belt or chain runs onto a pulley, roller, or sprocket, or where a moving part meets a fixed part. OSHA's amputation guide lists take-up and discharge ends, belt wrap around pulleys, snub rollers, adjoining conveyors, and transfers. Nip points can draw in clothing, hair, or fingers. 1910.212 requires guarding against ingoing nip points.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is lockout/tagout for conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>LOTO isolates every energy source (electrical, pneumatic, hydraulic, gravity, stored mechanical energy) before servicing. 1910.147 requires documented procedures, inspection at least annually, and training. Each worker applies their own lock, and the employee who applied a lock removes it, with a documented exception when that employee is unavailable. Group lockout must give each worker protection equivalent to a personal lock.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How far apart should emergency stops be placed?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The B20.1 text we reviewed (2018 edition) sets no fixed spacing. It requires e-stop devices in drive areas, loading areas, transfer points, and other hazardous locations not otherwise guarded on remotely or automatically controlled conveyors, plus stop or e-stop pushbuttons at each operator control station. OSHA's amputation guide recommends pull cords along the full length of continuously accessible belts. NFPA 79 calls for red actuators on a yellow background, and e-stops must need a manual reset.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h2 id="design-safety-into-your-system">Design Safety Into Your System</h2>
<p>Safety is most effective, and least expensive, when designed into the conveyor system from the start. Use the <a href="/resources/conveyor-design-guide/">conveyor design guide</a> for a complete design process, or consult the <a href="/resources/conveyor-maintenance/">maintenance guide</a> for ongoing safety inspection procedures.</p>
